C16 survived several work units as "a systematic outlier round on P1.2 decode, about
34 percent high, in every log" -- recorded, printed per round rather than hidden in a
range, and unexplained. Each block below is a condition and the per-round ratio list
is the answer, so nothing here needs a threshold or a judgement call.

== C16: what makes the first rounds of P1.2 decode slow? ==

Per-round ffi/pb on P1.2 decode, 9 rounds. The steady value is about 0.54 and the
outlier value is about 0.67-0.69. It is USUALLY rounds 1 and 2 -- that is what made it
look like a warm-up -- but not always: on a busier box it also lands on later rounds.
What is invariant is the pair of values, not which round takes the high one.

-- is it the machine? Six runs, two linkages --
  shared, run 1                                  0.676,0.674,0.533,0.538,0.544,0.534,0.535,0.533,0.534
  shared, run 2                                  0.669,0.676,0.536,0.537,0.541,0.541,0.538,0.536,0.537
  shared, run 3                                  0.677,0.676,0.531,0.539,0.538,0.538,0.529,0.536,0.539
  static, run 1                                  0.685,0.686,0.546,0.545,0.559,0.550,0.553,0.552,0.549
  static, run 2                                  0.685,0.685,0.547,0.547,0.547,0.549,0.551,0.548,0.542
  static, run 3                                  0.680,0.681,0.544,0.582,0.556,0.552,0.553,0.545,0.548
  -> the two VALUES are deterministic and identical across linkages and runs. Load
     changes how many rounds take the high value, not what the high value is, so the
     first suspicion -- this container's own stale background pollers, stopped in
     W10 -- is refuted as the cause: an idle box still shows it.

-- is it the UTF-8 validator? bench_a17_scalarv is the same source with AK_UTF8=0 --
  AK_UTF8=1 (the table, shipped)                 0.681,0.696,0.535,0.544,0.534,0.547,0.541,0.541,0.547
  AK_UTF8=0 (the old scalar validator)           0.621,0.614,0.617,0.615,0.616,0.607,0.604,0.615,0.627
  -> NOT the same shape, and the difference is the point. With the old scalar
     validator the row is FLAT and slow (about 0.62); with the table validator it is
     fast (0.54) with the first two rounds at 0.67. The validator is not the CAUSE --
     the next block shows what is -- but it is why the outlier is visible: a decode
     dominated by a slow validator hides a fixed per-iteration allocator cost, and
     making the validator 2x faster turned that cost into a visible fraction.

-- is it the ALLOCATOR? glibc's mmap threshold is 128 KB by default and ADAPTS: when
   an mmap'd block is freed the threshold rises to its size, so later allocations of
   that size come from the recycled heap instead of a fresh mmap --
  default (adaptive threshold)                   0.663,0.659,0.530,0.537,0.529,0.532,0.529,0.532,0.535
  MALLOC_MMAP_THRESHOLD_=4096  (always mmap)     0.661,0.663,0.544,0.661,0.664,0.657,0.662,0.663,0.656
  MMAP_THRESHOLD_=512M (never mmap)              0.619,0.623,0.627,0.618,0.618,0.612,0.616,0.618,0.620
  ...and TRIM_THRESHOLD_=512M too                0.546,0.546,0.542,0.544,0.543,0.548,0.544,0.544,0.546
  -> pinning both thresholds removes the outlier AND keeps the steady state.
     Forcing always-mmap reproduces the outlier's VALUE in the later rounds too.

-- the mechanism, counted rather than inferred: minor page faults per process --
  default allocator   minor faults:     15946
  thresholds pinned   minor faults:      1480
  -> 10.8x more, which is the mmap path faulting the block in and giving it back

-- the residual, which this does NOT explain --
   Running another payload first sometimes removes it and sometimes does not:
  P1.1 then P1.2, run 1                          0.536,0.539,0.450,0.545,0.532,0.541,0.540,0.535,0.547
  P1.1 then P1.2, run 2                          0.533,0.541,0.537,0.549,0.532,0.542,0.548,0.538,0.545
  P1.1 then P1.2, run 3                          0.537,0.543,0.534,0.537,0.536,0.542,0.538,0.540,0.545
  P4.1 then P1.2, run 1                          0.681,0.672,0.536,0.542,0.541,0.542,0.537,0.540,0.536
  P4.1 then P1.2, run 2                          0.676,0.676,0.543,0.548,0.550,0.555,0.550,0.549,0.555
  P4.1 then P1.2, run 3                          0.668,0.672,0.544,0.545,0.548,0.542,0.545,0.548,0.548
   A predecessor does not reliably remove the outlier -- it MOVES it. With P1.1 first
   it may be absent, or appear at some later round; with P4.1 first it stays in rounds
   1 and 2. That is consistent with an allocator-state effect whose timing depends on
   what was allocated before, and NOT with a deterministic per-message warm-up, which
   is what a single run of this block first suggested.

== what this settles and what it does not ==

SETTLED, by removal: the outlier is page-fault cost on glibc's mmap path. Pinning the
two thresholds removes it and keeps the steady state, and the default allocator takes
an order of magnitude more minor faults. It is a property of the HOST ALLOCATOR under
this harness, not of the core, not of the ABI and not of the machine.

NOT SETTLED: exactly WHEN the threshold adapts. The outlier is usually rounds 1 and 2
but a different allocation history can move it to a later round or remove it, and this
does not predict which. What would settle it: a malloc hook logging size and
mmap-or-not per call, compared across predecessor orders. That is a morning's work
answering a question about glibc rather than about the ABI, which is why it stops here.
The practical consequence does not depend on the residual: the outlier is allocator
page-fault cost, it is bounded by the flat rows above, and two environment variables
remove it.

CONSEQUENCE for the figures: none are withdrawn. This slice reports min-of-rounds and
prints every round, so no published number was taken from an outlier round -- which is
what printing them was for. The caveat it adds is real though: a C++ consumer decoding
large messages pays an allocator cost that the default glibc tuning only amortises
after the first few messages, and two MALLOC_ environment variables remove it.
